# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
canalyzer_control_panel.py
==========================

Single-file bench control panel for Vector CANalyzer + ECU fault memory +
multi-CAN recording + RS52 power supply.

  * CANalyzer (COM automation): attach / launch, open / save / save-as
    configuration, start / stop / restart measurement, compile CAPL, system
    variables, signal read, Write window, native logging blocks (file name +
    trigger), show / hide, quit.
  * Bus monitor on ALL selected CAN channels (python-can, Vector XL driver):
    per-ID statistics (fixed trace), scrolling trace, one-shot / cyclic
    transmit, recording to BLF / ASC / CSV / TRC while CANalyzer keeps running.
  * Fault memory straight out of CANalyzer: the ECUs of the loaded
    configuration are detected over COM and their diagnostic description
    (CDD/ODX) does the addressing, so nothing has to be configured here.
    UDS 0x19 (count, list, supported, snapshot, extended data),
    0x14 clear, KWP2000 0x18 / 0x14, sessions, ECU reset, DTC setting,
    tester present, raw requests, export to Excel / CSV, DTC text import.
    Transport: built-in Python ISO-TP (classic CAN + CAN FD) or the CANalyzer
    diagnostic COM objects (ECU qualifier from the diagnostic configuration).
  * RS52 power supply (serial or TCP, ASCII): "OUTP 1" / "OUTP 0",
    "SO:VO <volts>", voltage limits, presets, ramp, power cycle, raw
    commands, emergency off, optional readback queries.
  * TRACE32 tab: attaches to a running TRACE32 through its remote API,
    runs PRACTICE commands, and samples symbols/variables added in the GUI -
    with a live strip chart, change detection and CSV recording.
  * CANalyzer windows inside this panel: make a CANalyzer window floating
    (IG block / generator, diagnostic console, fault memory, trace, ...) and
    embed it in the "CANalyzer windows" tab. Messages are then sent with the
    original IG block and diagnostics are shown in CANalyzer's own console -
    no duplicate configuration. Detaching puts every window back.
  * Every action is time-stamped into logs/events_<date>.log and can be
    mirrored into the CANalyzer Write window for correlation with the trace.

Usage
-----
    python canalyzer_control_panel.py              # start the GUI
    python canalyzer_control_panel.py --selftest   # built-in test suite (no hardware needed)

Dependencies (Windows bench PC)
-------------------------------
    pip install pywin32 python-can pyserial openpyxl

Bench notes
-----------
  * Start the CANalyzer measurement BEFORE opening the bus monitor so that
    CANalyzer keeps init access (bitrate ownership) of the channels. Leave
    the bitrate fields empty; the monitor then joins as listener/transmitter.
    "Auto-open bus monitor on measurement start" does exactly that.
  * Vector channels are resolved through the application name "CANalyzer"
    in Vector Hardware Config: channel 1 = CAN1, channel 2 = CAN2, ...
  * Try everything without hardware: Bus Monitor interface "virtual" +
    "Demo ECU", Power supply type "Simulated".
"""
